<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/docs/Snippet.abide'
import InvoicePreview from './InvoicePreview.abide'
import invoicePreviewSource from './InvoicePreview.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import invoiceDocumentSource from './InvoiceDocument.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="`render` takes a component already bound to its props">
        <Example
            id="render-preview"
            title="`render(component)` — an invoice preview from the component itself"
            summary="The invoice a customer will be sent, previewed before it is issued: the handler renders the app's own invoice component into a document of its own."
            files={{
                'InvoicePreview.abide': invoicePreviewSource,
                'InvoiceDocument.abide': invoiceDocumentSource,
            }}
        >
            <InvoicePreview />
        </Example>

        <p><em>0 copies of the markup</em> — the arm writes the invoice's markup a second time as a
        string, and escapes every value in it by hand.</p>

        <p><code>{'Document({ invoice })'}</code> is a <code>Component</code> — the component
        <strong>bound to its props</strong>, not invoked and not rendered. That is what makes the
        signature one argument rather than two, and the import is the same <code>.abide</code> a
        page would place with a tag.</p>

        <p>It produces the document's <strong>bytes</strong>, built whole before any of them are
        handed back (41.1) — the same thing the page pipeline sends. A caller that wants text
        decodes them, and a caller answering with them hands them to <code>page</code>, which takes
        them directly.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/answer-with-something-other-than-json')}>Response
        types</a> · <a href={url('/templates/reuse-a-piece-of-markup')}>Components</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A component written inside `<html>` writes the document">
        <p>The component <code>render</code> is given is the whole document when it is written
        inside <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>: its head, the attributes on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> and
        <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>, and its body. Written without one, it gets abide's own — an
        encoding and a viewport, and nothing else.</p>

        <p>An email body wants a document of its own — no bundle, no font preloads, inline styles —
        and that is the whole of what writing one means. Nothing else about the render changes. A
        component that writes <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> is a whole document, so placing one inside
        another is refused.</p>

        <Snippet source={invoiceDocumentSource} path="InvoiceDocument.abide" from={['<html lang']} />
    </Section>

    <Section title="The root layout writes the app's own document">
        <p>An app's pages are rendered the same way. The root <code>layout.abide</code> is their
        outermost component, so its <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> is the document they are served in:
        a <code>lang</code>, a favicon, a class on <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> that follows the theme.
        What abide contributes to it is the head merging, the mount meta and the bundle.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/set-the-title-and-social-preview')}>Head &amp; metadata</a>
        · <a href={url('/pages/serve-the-app-under-a-sub-path')}>Sub-path mounting</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/send-the-page-before-the-data-lands')}>Streaming HTML</a> —
            what the produced stream does</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/answer-with-something-other-than-json')}>Response types</a> —
            <code>page</code>, and what else a handler can return</li>
            <li><a href={url('/start/how-a-page-becomes-html')}>Request to paint</a> — where the
            page pipeline calls this</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
